[FINAL-TEXT] Confirming evidence in hand. Direct DROP rules on router6.eth2 match the ACL fingerprint. No further probes needed.

=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The iptables FORWARD chain on router6, specifically three DROP rules bound to its eth2 interface (the `r6_r3` uplink that connects router6 to router3 and thus to the rest of the campus network).

**Mechanism:** The three rules are:
```
-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP
-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP
-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP
```
The final rule is a blanket drop for every IP protocol. Together they silently discard all packets that arrive on eth2 — meaning every packet originating from the wider campus (via router3) and destined for the 172.18.51.x subnet (switch10: pc15–pc18) or the switch11 laptops is dropped without generating an ICMP unreachable reply. That silent drop is why ping from pc4 or pc11 to 172.18.51.15 times out rather than receiving an unreachable response.

**Localization:** router6, eth2. All other interfaces and devices are clean; infra_sweep flagged only router6.

**Root cause:** iptables ACL on router6 applies a policy of DROP for all forwarded traffic ingressing on eth2, completely severing reachability between the mswitch2/switch10/switch11 segment and the rest of the network.

**Fix:** Remove the three rules:
```
iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP
iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP
iptables -D FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP
```
This restores bidirectional routed connectivity between the two campus halves.
=== END ===